```
ROLE: theory_skeptic  VOTE: red

MUNDANE_EXPLANATIONS:
- The "~100% miss gap" is a textbook consequence of Jaccard under union normalization, NOT an empirical discovery. For an item of length s embedded in a doc of length D with otherwise-disjoint vocab, Jaccard(shingles(doc), shingles(item)) ≈ s/(s+D−overlap) ≈ span_ratio. So span_ratio≤0.1 ⇒ Jaccard≤~0.09 < any sane near-dup threshold (0.3+) BY DEFINITION. The paper measures Jaccard≈0.09 at span_ratio=0.1 and confirms exactly this closed-form prediction (RESULTS.md, "Reason measured directly"). The result is "expected complexity," not emergent.
- "Document-level dedup misses embedded spans" is the DESIGNED behavior of whole-document near-dup tools; calling this a "measured gap" against a different tool (n-gram contamination check) compares a hammer to a screwdriver — the delta reflects tool-purpose mismatch, not a hidden flaw in dedup pipelines.
- The "0% reported vs 20% true" headline is a tautological restatement: if detector recall=0 on a class, reported rate=0 by arithmetic. No additional information beyond recall=0.

CONFOUNDS:
- Simulation uses DISTINCT item vocab slice — passage-level precision is 1.0 trivially (admitted). The ONE meaningful precision test (partial-quote at 0.55–0.57) uses synthetic 90% quotes; real-world citation/quote-length/template distributions are unmeasured, so the precision boundary at span_thresh=0.95 is calibrated against an artificial confound, not the empirical FP distribution.
- "Standard practice = document-level near-dedup of training corpora" conflates CORPUS-dedup (whose goal is removing duplicate docs, not catching contamination) with contamination-DETECTION (whose published pipelines, e.g. GPT-3 13-gram, already operate at n-gram granularity). The claim sets up a strawman baseline that no contamination-detection paper actually uses.
- "Innocent" docs in the precision test have NO common-template / boilerplate content beyond Zipfian draws + a planted quote; real corpora have templates, license headers, repeated citations whose 8-gram structure could inflate FP further.

ASSUMPTIONS_UNTESTED:
- Item vocab is distinct from corpus vocab (real MMLU/GSM8K share heavy common-English vocabulary; n-gram FP profile will differ).
- MinHash-LSH (the named "standard") is approximated by raw Jaccard threshold; LSH-banding parameters and shingle-size choices change recall on partial overlaps and were NOT swept.
- Real contamination's paraphrase distribution (the load-bearing scoping variable; admitted untested and explicitly identified as deciding whether the result matters at scale).
- That doc_thresh=0.3 is "lenient" enough to represent a stress test — production dedup pipelines sometimes use stricter or different metrics (containment, Broder resemblance with weighted shingles) where containment(item, doc)=1.0 even when Jaccard=0.09. Containment-based dedup (a known variant) would catch exactly this case — UNTESTED, and would invert the headline.

STATISTICAL_GAPS:
- "Recall=0.00, recall=1.00" are point estimates with no CI; with 60 items × 7 seeds the std reported is fine for stability but Wilson CIs around 0.00/1.00 should be reported, especially because the headline is "100% gap" not ">20%."
- Precision 0.55–0.57 at low thresholds has only 7 seeds × small confounded set; no CI, no power analysis.
- No sensitivity to ngram_k (fixed at 8). The verbatim/paraphrase boundary is entirely a function of k — sweeping k would show the gap is parameterized by k, not intrinsic.
- No comparison to the OBVIOUS alternative document-level metric (CONTAINMENT = |A∩B|/|min(A,B)|), which is also published and would close the gap analytically. Omitting the strongest competitor inflates the apparent novelty of the gap.

RATIONALE: The headline empirical result (~100% miss at span_ratio≤0.1, threshold-robust) is predicted in closed form by basic Jaccard set arithmetic — and the paper itself derives the predicted Jaccard≈0.09 and confirms it (RESULTS.md, the union-normalization paragraph). A measurement that confirms textbook math to 3 decimals is not a quantified discovery; it is calibration. The "novel contribution" framing in the packet ("MEASURED GAP + rate effect + precision boundary") survives only at the precision-boundary finding (span_thresh~0.95 keeps precision=1.0 under a partial-quote confound while recall stays 1.0) — that single result IS empirical and not predicted analytically, but it is one calibration knob, not a "granularity gap" measurement, and it is not what the claim text foregrounds. Additionally: (i) the comparison conflates corpus-dedup with contamination-detection — the GPT-3 13-gram / PaLM 8-gram baselines are passage-level by design, so "doc-level reports 0%" attacks a strawman no contamination-detection pipeline actually deploys; (ii) the obvious competitor metric (CONTAINMENT, well-known, would catch span_ratio≤0.1 trivially) is not in the sweep, so the "doc-level granularity is fundamentally blind" framing is unsupported — it shows only that ONE doc-level metric (Jaccard with union normalization) is blind, which is again predicted analytically. Honest negative on paraphrase further restricts the regime to verbatim-only, which is the regime where the gap is most easily predicted from theory and where the n-gram-checks-find-more folklore is most-cited. Net: the claim's load-bearing measurement reduces to textbook arithmetic + one calibration knob; the framing inflates a predicted result into a novel quantified contribution. <cite>RESULTS.md "union normalization … 43/490 ≈ 0.09"; packet PRIOR-ART CAVEAT admitting GPT-3 13-gram / PaLM 8-gram / Lee et al. 2021 are passage-level baselines; absence of containment-metric sweep; paraphrase collapse documented in RESULTS.md "NOT held / honest boundary".</cite>
```
